# 6. Difference Between Largest and Smallest

### 🎯 Problem

Find the difference between the largest and smallest elements.

### 💻 Code

```java
package p1;

public class DiffBetweenSmallAndBiggest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

        int largest = arr[0];
        int smallest = arr[0];

        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {

            if (arr[i] > largest) {
                largest = arr[i];
            }

            if (arr[i] < smallest) {
                smallest = arr[i];
            }
        }

        int difference = largest - smallest;

        System.out.println("Difference = " + difference);
    }
}
```

### 🖥️ Output

```text
Difference = 40
```

### 🔍 Calculation

```text
Largest = 50
Smallest = 10

Difference = 50 - 10
           = 40
```

### 🧠 Important Pattern

You can find both maximum and minimum in **one loop**.

### ⏱️ Complexity

* Time: `O(n)`
* Space: `O(1)`

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 7. Average of Array Elements

### 🎯 Problem

Find the average of all elements in an array.

### 💻 Code

```java
package p1;

public class AverageOfElements {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

        int sum = 0;
        int count = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            sum = sum + arr[i];
            count++;
        }

        double average = (double) sum / count;

        System.out.println("Average = " + average);
    }
}
```

### 🖥️ Output

```text
Average = 30.0
```

### 🔍 Calculation

```text
Sum = 10 + 20 + 30 + 40 + 50
    = 150

Count = 5

Average = 150 / 5
        = 30.0
```

### 🧠 Important

If both operands are `int`:

```java
int result = 5 / 2;
```

Result:

```text
2
```

For decimal division:

```java
double result = (double) 5 / 2;
```

Result:

```text
2.5
```

### ⏱️ Complexity

* Time: `O(n)`
* Space: `O(1)`

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 8. Count Elements Greater Than Average

### 🎯 Problem

Count how many elements are greater than the average.

### 💻 Code

```java
package p1;

public class ElementsGreaterThanAverage {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

        int sum = 0;
        int count = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            sum = sum + arr[i];
            count++;
        }

        int average = sum / count;

        int greaterCount = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            if (arr[i] > average) {
                greaterCount++;
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Count = " + greaterCount);
    }
}
```

### 🖥️ Output

```text
Count = 2
```

### 🔍 Dry Run

Average:

```text
150 / 5 = 30
```

Then:

```text
10 → ❌
20 → ❌
30 → ❌
40 → ✅
50 → ✅
```

Therefore:

```text
Count = 2
```

### 🧠 Important Pattern

This problem uses **two passes**:

```text
Pass 1 → Calculate average
Pass 2 → Count elements greater than average
```

### ⏱️ Complexity

* Time: `O(n)`
* Space: `O(1)`

━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━

# 9. Find Second Smallest Element

### 🎯 Problem

Find the second smallest element.

### 💻 Code

```java
package p1;

public class SecondSmallest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

        int smallest = arr[0];
        int secondSmallest = arr[1];

        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {

            if (arr[i] < smallest) {
                secondSmallest = smallest;
                smallest = arr[i];
            }
        }

        System.out.println("Second Smallest = " + secondSmallest);
    }
}
```

### 🖥️ Output

```text
Second Smallest = 20
```

### 🔑 Important Pattern

When a new smallest is found:

```java
secondSmallest = smallest;
smallest = arr[i];
```

The **old smallest becomes the new second smallest**.

### ⚠️ Note

This beginner version works for the array used in today's exercise. Later, we'll learn a more robust second-smallest algorithm that handles arbitrary unsorted arrays and duplicate/edge cases correctly.

### ⏱️ Complexity

* Time: `O(n)`
* Space: `O(1)`

