# 10. Check if Array is Sorted in Ascending Order

### 🎯 Problem

Check whether an array is sorted in ascending order.

### 💻 Code

```java
package p1;

public class SortingInAscOrder {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] arr = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};

        boolean sorted = true;

        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length - 1; i++) {

            if (arr[i] > arr[i + 1]) {
                sorted = false;
                break;
            }
        }

        if (sorted) {
            System.out.println("Array is sorted");
        } else {
            System.out.println("Array is not sorted");
        }
    }
}
```

### 🖥️ Output

```text
Array is sorted
```

### 🔍 Dry Run

```text
10 > 20 → false
20 > 30 → false
30 > 40 → false
40 > 50 → false
```

No violation was found, so the array is sorted.

### 🧠 Why `arr.length - 1`?

We compare:

```java
arr[i]
```

with:

```java
arr[i + 1]
```

For an array of length `5`, the last index is `4`.

If `i = 4`, then:

```java
arr[i + 1]
arr[5]
```

would cause an `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`.

Therefore:

```java
i < arr.length - 1
```

### 🔑 Important Pattern

```java
boolean sorted = true;
```

Assume the array is sorted.

If we find a violation:

```java
if (arr[i] > arr[i + 1]) {
    sorted = false;
    break;
}
```

### ⏱️ Complexity

* Time: `O(n)`
* Space: `O(1)`

